  "key_findings": [
    "CRITICAL: Naive average-based consensus FAILS with any latency >= 1 tick. Only latency=0 converges. Stale peer reports create destructive corrections that push clocks AWAY from consensus.",
    "Larger \u03b4 makes divergence WORSE (not better), because stale-data corrections are amplified by the larger clamping window. Best strategy with latency>0 is smallest \u03b4 (1/64), which limits damage from bad corrections.",
    "Hypothesis REJECTED: optimal \u03b4 does not scale linearly with latency because no \u03b4 achieves convergence with latency>0. The system needs a latency-aware correction protocol (e.g., timestamp-based offset estimation, Cristian's algorithm, or PTP-style round-trip measurement) rather than naive averaging.",
    "Latency=0 achieves drift_ss=0.2515 regardless of \u03b4. Latency=1 (minimum nonzero) jumps to drift_ss=8.8072. This is a phase transition, not a gradual degradation."
